for its victims human trafficking is  physically and  mentally traumatizing and sometimes even  deadly  for criminal criminal gangs it's highly  lucrative the  main smuggling routes around the world  are through latin america  and the mediterranean and asia gangs  often force their victims  into prostitution or slave labor in the  service industries  in recent weeks police have been  cracking down on the 150 billion dollar  black market and the alliance against  trafficking in person says  the only way to stop it is to try to  stamp out the demand for  cheap sex dw correspondent young philip  schultz  went to italy on the trail of the  nigerian mafia  he spoke to traffickers and to some of  the women they exploited  these scenes could have been filmed at  countless industrial areas in italy  on any given night young nigerian women  working the street almost none of them  of their own free will  at rome central station we meet david  he says he was a member of the nigerian  mafia  until he witnessed the brutal murder of  a woman forced to work as a prostitute  they cut this person into pieces then  they package it so for example  if we are ten people that know the dead  body  you carry one killer i carry one kilo he  carry one killer  everybody will go and dispose it you can  never trace the person you can never  have the person again  the person is missing it's gone  one of the nigerian mafia's bases is  said to be the southern italian village  of voltorno  often a nigerian madam is in charge of a  group of young women  behind her a well-organized network of  violent operatives  smuggling young women from africa to  europe  juliet osabo arrived in italy aboard a  migrant boat  four years ago she saw first-hand how  the human traffickers and the madames  operate  they beat the girl like are you ready to  pay her the money or not  and if the guy says no i'm tired  i i don't want to do this work anymore  they continue to beat the girl  no food no water they locked the girl  inside the room  for four days until they are able to  conquer  her juliet managed to escape the  traffickers and found refuge at a  women's center for migrants  she believes most women like her fall  victim before they even leave their home  countries  smugglers lure young women to europe  with lies and promises  on arrival they're told they are  thousands of euros in debt and have to  pay it off  sergio natsaro is an adviser to the  anti-mafia commission  in the italian parliament he says the  italian state  now treats the nigerian mafia the same  as the italian mob  yes they are mafia it's under the code  law 416 b's  they have a structure like mafia so the  code of silence  violence intimidation psychological  pressure  revenge and so on and so on ex-mafia  member david says the nigerian groups  are now operating right across europe  and they all have one thing in common  violence he shows us a gruesome video of  men beating a woman  david says she refused to work on the  streets so they tortured her to death  most of them they were ghosts they are  not registered  nobody knows about them the government  don't know anything about them they are  here  they don't have document they don't have  anything so if they die  it's very difficult for the government  to know that this person has been  missing  juliet has since been registered with  the authorities  she now has a visa and a job at a local  restaurant  but she knows she's one of the lucky  ones as long as there's a demand for  cheap sex  the mafia will keep exploiting young  women and their search for a supposedly  better life will continue  to end on the streets
 

